> [!NOTE] Status
> This example needs pieces that don't exist yet: seeded sampling, an HMC sampler, and a
> positive-reals manifold ([What exists today](#what-exists-today)). It is here to show the kind of
> problem skoro is aimed at, and to keep the missing pieces in view.

How strongly does the climate respond to a push? A two-layer energy-balance model summarizes
global warming with a handful of physical parameters: a feedback $\lambda$, an exchange coefficient
$\gamma$, and two heat capacities. Calibrating them against the observational record means a
posterior over parameters that are **positive**, an observation-noise covariance that is a
**triangular factor**, and a likelihood that runs through an **ODE solve**. The last makes gradients
the interesting part.

## The model

Surface temperature anomaly $T$ and deep-ocean anomaly $T_D$ respond to radiative forcing $F(t)$:

$$
\begin{aligned}
C\,\dot T &= F(t) - \lambda T - \gamma\,(T - T_D), \\
C_D\,\dot T_D &= \gamma\,(T - T_D).
\end{aligned}
$$

With $\theta = (\lambda, \gamma, C, C_D) \in \Rpos^4$ and the state $\state = (T, T_D)$, one
integration step over a year is a map $\state_k = \flow(\state_{k-1}, F_k)$. The system is linear in
$\state$, so a fixed-step Runge–Kutta step is a short composition of matrix products, and it is
differentiable with the operations skoro already has.

Each year observes the surface temperature and the top-of-atmosphere imbalance
$N = F - \lambda T$:

$$
\obs_k \mid \state_k, \theta, L \sim \calN\!\left( \begin{bmatrix} T_k \\ F_k - \lambda T_k \end{bmatrix},\ LL^\top \right),
\qquad
\log\theta \sim \calN(\mu_\theta, \Sigma_\theta),
\qquad
L \sim \pi(L).
$$

