## Sampling the posterior

The posterior $p(\theta, L \mid \obs)$ has no closed form. Hamiltonian Monte Carlo samples it by
simulating a particle moving on the parameter manifold, with potential energy $-\log p$:

- the gradient comes from `log_density_ord1`, back through every integration step;
- each leapfrog position update is a retraction, `retract_flat`, so $\theta$ stays positive and $L$
  stays triangular without clipping;
- the Metropolis correction uses `log_density`.

The Riemannian variant uses the Fisher information $\calI(\theta)$ as a position-dependent mass
matrix, which adapts to the very different scales of $\lambda$ and $C_D$. Skoro keeps the Fisher
root as a differentiable node for exactly this reason
([Fisher scoring](../../concepts/fisher-scoring/#beyond-optimization)).

